+# P31 client half — `/verify` must not answer "not found" when it was refused
+
+**⚠ FILE-OWNERSHIP EXCEPTION, STATED FIRST BECAUSE IT IS THE FIRST THING A REVIEWER SHOULD SEE**
+
+`src/**` is **D2's**, exclusively. The rule is not a formality — it is what makes two developers on one repository possible, and it says *not even a typo*. This branch changes two files under `src/` and was written by **D1**, on the Auditor's explicit written instruction of **2026-09-03**:
+
+> *"Write the CLIENT half as a separate patch — `src/pages/VerifyCertificate.tsx` must distinguish a 42501 permission error from an empty result… That is the change that unblocks 0001."*
+
+Recorded, not assumed. Three consequences the Auditor should weigh before this lands:
+
+1. **D2 must own and sign off this change.** D1 cannot see D2's session. If D2 has `VerifyCertificate.tsx` open or modified right now, this patch conflicts and **D2's version wins.**
+2. **It is delivered as a patch on its own branch**, `d1/P31-client-half-20260903`, so it is trivially discardable and does not sit inside a D1 unit.
+3. **It is not merged by D1 and not promoted by D1.** As with every unit here.
+
+---
+
+## 1 · The defect
+
+`src/pages/VerifyCertificate.tsx`, before:
+
+```js
+const { data, error } = await supabase.rpc("search_certificates", { ... });
+if (error || !data || data.length === 0) { setNotFound(true); }
+```
+
+One branch for three different worlds. After P31 revokes `EXECUTE` from `anon`, a `42501 permission denied` takes the same path as an empty result and the page renders **"No Certificates Found"**.
+
+`/verify` is public and unguarded (`src/App.tsx:410`). So the page tells a real certificate holder, searching their own name, that their certificate does not exist. **A confident wrong answer is worse than an error**, because it looks like a correct answer and never reaches support.
+
+## 2 · The change
+
+| file | what |
+|---|---|
+| `src/pages/verifyCertificateErrors.ts` | **new** — `isSearchUnavailableError()`, a pure classifier |
+| `src/pages/VerifyCertificate.tsx` | check the refusal **first**; new `searchUnavailable` state; new panel; `notFound` suppressed while it is set |
+| `src/pages/__tests__/VerifyCertificateSearchUnavailable.test.tsx` | **new** — 9 cases |
+
+Copy is the Auditor's, verbatim: **"This search is no longer available, please verify by certificate ID"**, under a *Search Unavailable* heading — deliberately neutral, not an error style, because from the visitor's side nothing has gone wrong.
+
+**Two codes, not one.** `42501` is PostgREST reaching the function and Postgres refusing the caller. `PGRST202` is PostgREST's schema cache no longer listing the function for that role, so the call never reaches Postgres. Which one appears depends on whether the cache has reloaded since the revoke. **Both mean the same thing to a visitor, and handling only `42501` would leave the original defect live for whichever window the other one covers.** A message-substring fallback is a deliberate last resort for a proxy that strips the code — codes are the contract, prose is not.
+
+**The classifier lives in its own module** because ESLint's `react-refresh/only-export-components` says so, and because a control that can only be exercised through a full page render is a control that stops being run.
+
+## 3 · Shown failing first (C-34)
+
+Same test file, classifier module in place, **only the component reverted** — so the failures isolate the collapsed branch and nothing else:
+
+```
+✓ isSearchUnavailableError · 42501                                     (4 classifier cases pass — not the defect)
+× shows the unavailable message, NOT 'not found', on 42501
+× shows the unavailable message on PGRST202 as well
+✓ still says 'not found' for a genuine empty result                    ← passes BEFORE and AFTER, on purpose
+✓ still says 'not found' for an unrelated error                        ← passes BEFORE and AFTER, on purpose
+× clears the unavailable panel when switching to By Certificate ID
+
+Tests  3 failed | 6 passed (9)
+```
+
+After the fix: **9 passed (9)**.
+
+The two tests that pass in both states are there deliberately. **A fix that turned every empty search into "unavailable" would satisfy the first assertion and be a worse bug than the one it replaced**; those two are what stop it.
+
+Against the fully unpatched tree the same file reads **7 failed | 2 passed**, the extra four being the classifier cases against a symbol that did not exist yet.
+
+**The test was wrong once, and the fixed component caught it.** `getByText(/By Certificate ID/i)` matched twice — the mode button *and* the words "verify by certificate ID" inside the new message. The test was corrected to a role query; the component was not touched to suit it. Recorded because the opposite move is the mistake this discipline exists to prevent.
+
+## 4 · Build health
+
+| check | result |
+|---|---|
+| `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.app.json` | clean |
+| `eslint` on the three files | **0 errors, 2 warnings** |
+| the 9 new cases | pass |
+
+Both remaining warnings are **pre-existing**, measured by linting the unmodified file on `5cd5ba6`: an unused `eslint-disable` directive and a `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` warning on the existing auto-verify `useEffect`, at lines 122 and 127 before the change and 131 and 136 after. **This change adds no new lint warnings**, and I did not fix the two that were already there — they are D2's file and not this unit's business (Standing Rule: not even a typo).
+
+## 5 · What this does not do, and what it costs
+
+- **It does not gate the mode toggle.** The "By Name / Course" tab stays visible and the visitor still types a name before learning the search is closed. Hiding the tab when the RPC is known-refused would be better, and would mean not asking for a person's name at all. **Not done — it exceeds the instruction.** Offered as a follow-up for D2 to own.
+- **It is not proven in a browser.** These are jsdom tests. F-53 stands: curl is not a browser, and neither is jsdom. A device check on `/verify` belongs to D2.
+- **It does not verify against a real revoked function.** The error shapes are mocked from PostgREST's documented codes. **The first real proof is applying `20260910_0001` to staging and loading `/verify`** — which is the correct order and is the next step, not this one.
+- **It does not touch `verify_certificate`.** The by-ID path is unaffected, which is why the message can honestly point at it.
+
+## 6 · Status
+
+| class | applies to |
+|---|---|
+| **VERIFIED** | §3 and §4 — run personally on this branch, `vitest` and `tsc` and `eslint`, 2026-09-03 13:15Z–13:18Z |
+| **VERIFIED** | §1 — call site and route read from `src/` at ref `5cd5ba6` |
+| **INFERRED** | the exact error code PostgREST returns after the revoke. Documented behaviour, not observed on this database. Both plausible codes are handled; §5 says how to settle it. |
+| **BLOCKED** | merge — needs D2's ownership and sign-off, and the Auditor's record of the ownership exception |
+
+*D1 · Database & Runtime · 2026-09-03 · authored under the Auditor's written instruction into a file D1 does not own · no SQL apply · no grant altered on either lane.*
